1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:01,397
One wrong answer.

2
00:00:01,417 --> 00:00:04,625
The program prints a message, and then it
ends.

3
00:00:04,645 --> 00:00:06,822
The customer has to start again.

4
00:00:07,420 --> 00:00:09,596
Welcome back to Code Quest.

5
00:00:09,616 --> 00:00:11,060
This is Chapter 7.

6
00:00:11,100 --> 00:00:13,391
Today your programs learn to repeat.

7
00:00:13,411 --> 00:00:15,830
They keep going until the job is done.

8
00:00:15,867 --> 00:00:19,123
And an AI assistant will promise three
tries.

9
00:00:19,143 --> 00:00:21,453
Its loop will ask a fourth time.

10
00:00:22,053 --> 00:00:26,407
And a real cafe takes items until the
customer says: that is all.

11
00:00:26,427 --> 00:00:28,224
So here is today's mission.

12
00:00:28,244 --> 00:00:31,723
Asha's order loop asks for an item, again
and again.

13
00:00:31,767 --> 00:00:35,557
Coffee is not on the menu, so it says
sorry, and asks again.

14
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:40,591
When the customer types done, the loop
stops, and the program prints the items

15
00:00:40,611 --> 00:00:41,490
and the total.

16
00:00:41,533 --> 00:00:43,507
One loop, any number of items.

17
00:00:43,527 --> 00:00:44,503
Let's build it.

18
00:00:45,083 --> 00:00:48,373
Write a while loop that repeats while a
condition is True.

19
00:00:48,400 --> 00:00:51,130
Use a counter and a running total.

20
00:00:51,167 --> 00:00:54,397
Ask a question again until the answer is
valid.

21
00:00:54,433 --> 00:00:57,403
Stop a loop with a stop value, or with
break.

22
00:00:57,433 --> 00:01:02,030
And trace a loop in a table, to see why it
ends, or why it never does.

23
00:01:02,617 --> 00:01:04,245
Real programs repeat.

24
00:01:04,265 --> 00:01:07,306
A cash machine asks for your PIN again.

25
00:01:07,326 --> 00:01:11,858
A shop's till scans items until the
cashier presses Total.

26
00:01:11,878 --> 00:01:13,977
A game runs until you quit.

27
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:19,143
Each of these is a loop: a block of code
that runs again and again, until a

28
00:01:19,163 --> 00:01:20,520
condition says stop.

29
00:01:21,117 --> 00:01:24,673
A while loop checks a condition, the same
way as if.

30
00:01:24,693 --> 00:01:27,837
If it is True, Python runs the indented
block.

31
00:01:27,867 --> 00:01:33,060
But then, unlike if, Python goes back to
the top and checks the condition again.

32
00:01:33,080 --> 00:01:35,537
One run of the block is called a pass.

33
00:01:35,567 --> 00:01:40,772
When the condition is False, Python skips
the block, and continues after the loop.

34
00:01:41,387 --> 00:01:43,479
Here is the one rule of every loop.

35
00:01:43,499 --> 00:01:48,007
Something inside the block must change the
value that the condition checks.

36
00:01:48,033 --> 00:01:53,253
If nothing changes, the condition stays
True forever, and the loop never ends.

37
00:01:53,300 --> 00:01:57,017
Think of a waiter who asks every table:
anything else?

38
00:01:57,037 --> 00:02:02,692
Unlike a real waiter, Python never gets
tired, and never notices that something is

39
00:02:02,712 --> 00:02:03,107
wrong.

40
00:02:03,720 --> 00:02:04,425
Quick check.

41
00:02:04,445 --> 00:02:08,415
What must a line inside a while loop do,
so that the loop can end?

42
00:02:19,780 --> 00:02:19,897
C.

43
00:02:19,917 --> 00:02:22,695
Change a value that the condition checks.

44
00:02:22,715 --> 00:02:26,245
That is the only way the condition can
become False.

45
00:02:26,853 --> 00:02:27,950
Take a breather.

46
00:02:27,970 --> 00:02:29,973
Here is what you know so far.

47
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:33,965
Next, Asha serves her first tables, one
pass at a time.

48
00:02:38,717 --> 00:02:39,223
Step 1.

49
00:02:39,243 --> 00:02:41,179
Asha serves tables 1 to 3.

50
00:02:41,199 --> 00:02:44,941
Line 2 creates a counter, table, and sets
it to 1.

51
00:02:44,961 --> 00:02:48,477
A counter is a variable that counts the
passes.

52
00:02:48,500 --> 00:02:52,850
Line 3 is the loop: while table is less
than or equal to 3.

53
00:02:52,900 --> 00:02:55,068
Line 4 prints the table number.

54
00:02:55,088 --> 00:02:57,115
Line 5 adds 1 to the counter.

55
00:02:57,135 --> 00:03:01,420
That is the line that changes the value
the condition checks.

56
00:03:01,467 --> 00:03:05,142
Line 6 is not indented, so it is outside
the loop.

57
00:03:16,513 --> 00:03:19,353
Three times: tables 1, 2 and 3.

58
00:03:19,400 --> 00:03:21,892
After the third pass, table is 4.

59
00:03:21,912 --> 00:03:27,600
4 is not less than or equal to 3, so the
loop ends, and line 6 prints once.

60
00:03:28,183 --> 00:03:28,657
Step 2.

61
00:03:28,677 --> 00:03:31,335
This fixes the problem from Chapter 6.

62
00:03:31,355 --> 00:03:36,903
Line 2 asks once, before the loop, so
people has a value when line 3 checks it.

63
00:03:36,933 --> 00:03:40,177
Line 3: while people is less than 1.

64
00:03:40,197 --> 00:03:42,443
Line 4 explains the rule.

65
00:03:42,467 --> 00:03:45,044
And line 5 asks again, inside the loop.

66
00:03:45,064 --> 00:03:48,508
That new answer is the change that can end
the loop.

67
00:03:59,880 --> 00:04:00,342
Twice.

68
00:04:00,362 --> 00:04:02,993
Once for 0, and once for minus 2.

69
00:04:03,013 --> 00:04:04,680
Both are less than 1.

70
00:04:04,700 --> 00:04:07,112
Then 4 makes the condition False.

71
00:04:07,132 --> 00:04:10,207
The loop ends, and line 6 shows the table.

72
00:04:10,783 --> 00:04:11,346
Step 3.

73
00:04:11,366 --> 00:04:13,011
Asha adds up prices.

74
00:04:13,031 --> 00:04:19,005
Line 2 starts a running total at 0: a
variable that grows on every pass.

75
00:04:19,025 --> 00:04:21,003
Line 3 starts a counter.

76
00:04:21,033 --> 00:04:24,629
Line 4 asks for the first price, before
the loop.

77
00:04:24,649 --> 00:04:26,621
The user types 0 to finish.

78
00:04:26,641 --> 00:04:30,089
A value that means stop is called a stop
value.

79
00:04:30,109 --> 00:04:32,893
Line 5 loops while the price is not 0.

80
00:04:32,933 --> 00:04:35,526
Line 6 says total plus equals price.

81
00:04:35,546 --> 00:04:39,082
That is short for: total equals total plus
price.

82
00:04:39,102 --> 00:04:42,058
Line 7 adds 1 to the count, the same way.

83
00:04:42,078 --> 00:04:45,323
And minus equals takes away, in the same
way.

84
00:04:45,367 --> 00:04:50,768
And line 8 asks for the next price, as the
last line of the pass.

85
00:05:02,147 --> 00:05:05,767
Three items, and a total of 9.75.

86
00:05:05,800 --> 00:05:08,123
The stop value is not added.

87
00:05:08,143 --> 00:05:12,390
Line 5 checks the 0 first, so line 6 never
adds it.

88
00:05:12,983 --> 00:05:13,458
Step 4.

89
00:05:13,478 --> 00:05:15,083
Line 2 says while True.

90
00:05:15,103 --> 00:05:18,333
That condition never becomes False on its
own.

91
00:05:18,367 --> 00:05:24,397
Line 3 asks for an item, and cleans it
with strip and lower, as in Chapter 6.

92
00:05:24,433 --> 00:05:27,461
Lines 4 and 5: if the item is done, break.

93
00:05:27,481 --> 00:05:32,033
Break ends the loop immediately, and
Python continues after it.

94
00:05:43,413 --> 00:05:43,602
No.

95
00:05:43,622 --> 00:05:49,163
Strip and lower turn Done into done, so
break runs first, and line 6 is skipped.

96
00:05:49,200 --> 00:05:52,966
Then line 7 runs after the loop: order
closed.

97
00:05:53,587 --> 00:05:54,463
Quick check.

98
00:05:54,483 --> 00:05:55,732
Cups starts at 1.

99
00:05:55,752 --> 00:05:59,092
While cups is less than 5, cups goes up by
2.

100
00:05:59,112 --> 00:06:01,183
What is cups after the loop?

101
00:06:12,547 --> 00:06:12,720
A.

102
00:06:12,740 --> 00:06:12,914
5.

103
00:06:12,934 --> 00:06:14,851
1 becomes 3, then 5.

104
00:06:14,871 --> 00:06:18,628
5 is not less than 5, so the loop ends.

105
00:06:19,220 --> 00:06:19,800
Breather.

106
00:06:19,820 --> 00:06:22,000
Sit back, and take a slow breath.

107
00:06:22,033 --> 00:06:26,658
Next, a tool that shows exactly what a
loop does: the trace table.

108
00:06:31,383 --> 00:06:31,935
Step 5.

109
00:06:31,955 --> 00:06:33,322
Cups starts at 1.

110
00:06:33,342 --> 00:06:38,873
While cups is less than 20, line 4 doubles
it, and line 5 prints it.

111
00:06:38,900 --> 00:06:43,941
A trace table writes down the value of
each variable on every pass.

112
00:06:43,961 --> 00:06:45,301
Let's fill one in.

113
00:06:45,920 --> 00:06:46,506
Pass 1.

114
00:06:46,526 --> 00:06:47,371
Cups is 1.

115
00:06:47,391 --> 00:06:50,660
1 is less than 20, so it doubles to 2.

116
00:06:50,700 --> 00:06:52,905
Pass 2: 2 becomes 4.

117
00:06:52,925 --> 00:06:55,130
Pass 3: 4 becomes 8.

118
00:06:55,167 --> 00:06:56,247
Pass 4: 8 becomes 16.

119
00:06:56,267 --> 00:06:58,854
Now cups is 16 at the check.

120
00:06:58,874 --> 00:07:02,021
Fill in the next row in your head.

121
00:07:13,380 --> 00:07:13,608
32.

122
00:07:13,628 --> 00:07:19,730
16 is still less than 20, so pass 5 runs
completely, and doubles it to 32.

123
00:07:19,767 --> 00:07:22,590
Only then does the check find False.

124
00:07:22,610 --> 00:07:27,091
Python checks the condition only at the
top of each pass.

125
00:07:27,687 --> 00:07:34,792
And the real run agrees with the table: 2,
4, 8, 16, 32, then stopped at 32.

126
00:07:35,383 --> 00:07:36,827
Back to the mission.

127
00:07:36,847 --> 00:07:40,853
Lines 2 and 3 start a running total and a
counter at 0.

128
00:07:40,900 --> 00:07:43,936
Line 4 starts the loop with while True.

129
00:07:43,956 --> 00:07:48,480
Lines 5 to 7 ask for an item, clean it,
and break on done.

130
00:07:48,500 --> 00:07:51,861
Lines 8 to 13 are the decisions from
Chapter 6.

131
00:07:51,881 --> 00:07:55,890
Tea adds 3.5, cake adds 4, and each adds 1
to the count.

132
00:07:55,933 --> 00:07:58,900
Lines 14 and 15 catch anything else.

133
00:07:58,920 --> 00:08:02,634
And lines 16 and 17 run once, after the
loop.

134
00:08:14,013 --> 00:08:18,203
Coffee gets a sorry, and changes nothing.

135
00:08:18,233 --> 00:08:24,213
Two teas and a cake: 3 items, and 3.5 plus
4 plus 3.5 is 11.00.

136
00:08:24,233 --> 00:08:25,533
Mission complete!

137
00:08:25,553 --> 00:08:29,183
Next, Asha protects the How many teas
question.

138
00:08:29,203 --> 00:08:32,755
She uses an AI assistant, one stage at a
time.

139
00:08:33,350 --> 00:08:35,835
This chapter's AI skill is tracing.

140
00:08:35,855 --> 00:08:41,060
You ask the AI to trace a loop for one set
of answers, one line per pass.

141
00:08:41,100 --> 00:08:43,771
Then you run the same answers yourself.

142
00:08:43,791 --> 00:08:47,153
And the safety habits from Chapter 1 still
apply.

143
00:08:47,753 --> 00:08:48,749
Stage 1: plan.

144
00:08:48,769 --> 00:08:51,651
The number of teas must be from 1 to 10.

145
00:08:51,671 --> 00:08:57,383
Asha asks for the loop condition, the
value that must change, and test answers.

146
00:08:57,403 --> 00:08:58,253
No code yet.

147
00:08:58,300 --> 00:09:03,455
The AI names the condition, says the new
answer is what ends the loop, and lists

148
00:09:03,475 --> 00:09:07,982
test answers, including 1 and 10, the
points where the result changes.

149
00:09:08,002 --> 00:09:10,117
These are called boundary values.

150
00:09:10,720 --> 00:09:11,852
Stage 2: write.

151
00:09:11,872 --> 00:09:17,538
Asha writes the loop herself, and asks the
AI to trace it for 0, 12 and 3.

152
00:09:17,558 --> 00:09:21,610
The AI traces two passes, then a check
that is False.

153
00:09:21,633 --> 00:09:24,453
Asha traced it too, and she runs it.

154
00:09:24,473 --> 00:09:25,715
All three agree.

155
00:09:26,320 --> 00:09:27,569
Stage 3: generate.

156
00:09:27,589 --> 00:09:31,515
Asha wants a limit: at most 3 tries, and
then use 1 tea.

157
00:09:31,535 --> 00:09:33,700
She asks what 0, 0 and 0 print.

158
00:09:33,733 --> 00:09:37,389
The AI says: three questions, then too
many tries.

159
00:09:37,409 --> 00:09:39,373
It gives no warning at all.

160
00:09:39,400 --> 00:09:45,684
Look at the condition on line 4, and
remember the one rule of every loop.

161
00:09:57,047 --> 00:09:58,039
None of them.

162
00:09:58,059 --> 00:10:01,701
Lines 5 and 6 print and ask, but tries
stays 1.

163
00:10:01,721 --> 00:10:03,259
Asha suspects a bug.

164
00:10:03,279 --> 00:10:05,518
Now she proves it with tests.

165
00:10:06,120 --> 00:10:07,092
Stage 4: test.

166
00:10:07,112 --> 00:10:11,625
Asha asks for edge cases: unusual answers
that can break a rule.

167
00:10:11,645 --> 00:10:14,600
And the exact output each one should give.

168
00:10:14,633 --> 00:10:15,805
The AI lists five.

169
00:10:15,825 --> 00:10:20,174
1 and 10 are boundary values, the points
where the result changes.

170
00:10:20,194 --> 00:10:24,543
The last case is three wrong answers:
three questions, then 1 tea.

171
00:10:24,567 --> 00:10:27,569
She runs every case on the AI's code.

172
00:10:27,589 --> 00:10:30,836
Here is case 4: three questions, then 5.

173
00:10:30,856 --> 00:10:31,653
It passes.

174
00:10:32,253 --> 00:10:33,621
Cases 1 to 4 pass.

175
00:10:33,641 --> 00:10:38,013
1 and 10 are accepted, and wrong answers
are asked again.

176
00:10:38,033 --> 00:10:39,244
But case 5 fails.

177
00:10:39,264 --> 00:10:42,790
Three wrong answers should end in too many
tries.

178
00:10:42,810 --> 00:10:45,033
The program asks a fourth time.

179
00:10:45,653 --> 00:10:46,901
Here is case 5.

180
00:10:46,921 --> 00:10:48,928
She types 0 three times.

181
00:10:48,948 --> 00:10:51,125
The program does not stop.

182
00:10:51,145 --> 00:10:52,983
It asks a fourth time.

183
00:10:53,033 --> 00:10:54,979
She types 5, and it takes it.

184
00:10:54,999 --> 00:10:56,538
The limit never worked.

185
00:10:56,558 --> 00:10:59,114
With more zeros, it would ask forever.

186
00:10:59,720 --> 00:11:00,727
Stage 5: debug.

187
00:11:00,747 --> 00:11:03,466
Asha pastes the loop and the failed run.

188
00:11:03,486 --> 00:11:09,080
She asks for a trace table first, then the
value that never changes, then the fix.

189
00:11:09,100 --> 00:11:11,055
The trace shows it clearly.

190
00:11:11,075 --> 00:11:12,883
Tries is 1 on every pass.

191
00:11:12,903 --> 00:11:18,149
Nothing inside the loop changes it, so
tries less than 3 is always True.

192
00:11:18,169 --> 00:11:21,513
The fix: tries plus equals 1, inside the
loop.

193
00:11:22,120 --> 00:11:25,620
Asha writes the trace as a table, like
step 5.

194
00:11:25,640 --> 00:11:28,680
Three passes, and tries is 1 every time.

195
00:11:28,700 --> 00:11:34,249
That matches her run: one first question,
plus three passes, is four questions.

196
00:11:34,269 --> 00:11:36,223
The trace and the run agree.

197
00:11:36,820 --> 00:11:38,909
Here is the fix on line 7.

198
00:11:38,929 --> 00:11:44,180
Now tries goes 1, 2, 3, and the third
wrong answer ends the loop.

199
00:11:44,200 --> 00:11:46,370
Too many tries, using 1 tea.

200
00:11:46,390 --> 00:11:50,123
Exactly what the AI promised in the first
place.

201
00:11:50,720 --> 00:11:52,777
A fix can break something else.

202
00:11:52,797 --> 00:11:56,060
So Asha runs every case again, on the
fixed code.

203
00:11:56,100 --> 00:11:57,188
All five pass.

204
00:11:57,208 --> 00:11:58,613
Now she trusts it.

205
00:11:59,220 --> 00:12:00,404
Stage 6: improve.

206
00:12:00,424 --> 00:12:05,928
Asha asks for at most two readability
improvements that do not change how many

207
00:12:05,948 --> 00:12:06,920
times it asks.

208
00:12:06,967 --> 00:12:09,997
Suggestion 1, a comment, helps a reader.

209
00:12:10,017 --> 00:12:16,099
Suggestion 2 says: change less than 3 to
less than or equal to 3, for three full

210
00:12:16,119 --> 00:12:16,557
tries.

211
00:12:16,600 --> 00:12:18,417
Asha does not accept it yet.

212
00:12:18,437 --> 00:12:20,320
She will count the questions.

213
00:12:20,920 --> 00:12:26,663
Here is the suggested condition on line 4:
tries less than or equal to 3.

214
00:12:26,683 --> 00:12:28,400
Trace it in your head.

215
00:12:28,420 --> 00:12:30,532
Tries is 1, then 2, then 3.

216
00:12:41,913 --> 00:12:42,671
Four times.

217
00:12:42,691 --> 00:12:47,763
When tries is 3, the condition is still
True, so there is one more pass.

218
00:12:47,800 --> 00:12:51,851
And Asha had said: do not change how many
times it asks.

219
00:12:51,871 --> 00:12:53,305
The AI ignored that.

220
00:12:53,325 --> 00:12:56,867
She keeps less than 3, and adds only the
comment.

221
00:12:57,487 --> 00:12:58,324
Quick check.

222
00:12:58,344 --> 00:12:59,610
A loop never ends.

223
00:12:59,630 --> 00:13:01,968
What do you ask the AI for first?

224
00:13:13,347 --> 00:13:13,478
B.

225
00:13:13,498 --> 00:13:15,515
A trace table of each pass.

226
00:13:15,535 --> 00:13:18,156
It shows which value never changes.

227
00:13:18,753 --> 00:13:19,391
Breather.

228
00:13:19,411 --> 00:13:23,703
Asha caught two AI mistakes, with a trace,
and by counting.

229
00:13:23,733 --> 00:13:27,168
Next, the four mistakes beginners make
most with loops.

230
00:13:27,188 --> 00:13:29,303
You have already seen one of them.

231
00:13:34,050 --> 00:13:34,716
Mistake 1.

232
00:13:34,736 --> 00:13:38,623
The condition on line 2 is checked before
the first pass.

233
00:13:38,643 --> 00:13:40,680
But answer does not exist yet.

234
00:13:40,700 --> 00:13:42,968
Python stops with a Name Error.

235
00:13:42,988 --> 00:13:48,945
The fix: ask once before the loop, as in
step 2, or use while True with break, as

236
00:13:48,965 --> 00:13:49,683
in step 4.

237
00:13:50,287 --> 00:13:51,287
Now you try.

238
00:13:51,307 --> 00:13:54,177
Asha wants to serve tables 1 to 3.

239
00:13:54,197 --> 00:13:56,472
Compare line 3 with step 1.

240
00:14:07,847 --> 00:14:08,446
Table 3.

241
00:14:08,466 --> 00:14:14,257
Less than 3 is False when table is 3, so
the loop stops one pass too early.

242
00:14:14,300 --> 00:14:16,682
No error, only a missing table.

243
00:14:16,702 --> 00:14:19,394
This is called an off-by-one error.

244
00:14:19,414 --> 00:14:21,951
The fix: less than or equal to 3.

245
00:14:22,553 --> 00:14:24,857
Mistake 3 is the one the AI made.

246
00:14:24,877 --> 00:14:30,280
Nothing inside this loop changes table, so
it prints table 1 again and again,

247
00:14:30,300 --> 00:14:30,843
forever.

248
00:14:30,867 --> 00:14:36,219
If a program never stops, click in the
terminal and press Control and C together.

249
00:14:36,239 --> 00:14:39,337
Python stops with a Keyboard Interrupt
message.

250
00:14:39,367 --> 00:14:44,094
The fix: add the line that changes the
counter, inside the loop.

251
00:14:44,687 --> 00:14:45,306
One more.

252
00:14:45,326 --> 00:14:48,217
The loop stops on done, in small letters.

253
00:14:48,237 --> 00:14:50,915
The user types Done, with a capital D.

254
00:15:02,280 --> 00:15:05,330
Done is added to the order as if it were
an item.

255
00:15:05,350 --> 00:15:08,400
The loop keeps going until done in small
letters.

256
00:15:08,433 --> 00:15:13,682
The fix is the one from step 4: compare
the answer after strip and lower.

257
00:15:14,287 --> 00:15:15,190
Last breather.

258
00:15:15,210 --> 00:15:18,357
Now you know the four most common loop
mistakes.

259
00:15:18,400 --> 00:15:21,710
Two questions to think about on your own
come next.

260
00:15:26,450 --> 00:15:26,849
First.

261
00:15:26,869 --> 00:15:29,086
The cafe kettle needs 5 minutes.

262
00:15:29,106 --> 00:15:33,769
How would you count down from 5 to 1, and
then print: tea is ready?

263
00:15:45,147 --> 00:15:49,772
Think about the value that changes on each
pass, and its direction.

264
00:15:49,792 --> 00:15:51,436
Minus equals takes away.

265
00:15:52,053 --> 00:15:52,490
Second.

266
00:15:52,510 --> 00:15:57,778
How could Asha's order loop stop after 10
items, even if the customer never types

267
00:15:57,798 --> 00:15:58,104
done?

268
00:16:09,480 --> 00:16:12,447
Trace your loop with 10 items before you
run it.

269
00:16:13,050 --> 00:16:14,070
Let's recap.

270
00:16:14,100 --> 00:16:17,630
A while loop repeats its block while the
condition is True.

271
00:16:17,667 --> 00:16:23,387
A line inside the loop must change the
value that the condition checks.

272
00:16:23,433 --> 00:16:26,080
A counter counts passes, and a running
total grows.

273
00:16:26,100 --> 00:16:28,447
Plus equals and minus equals are short
forms.

274
00:16:28,467 --> 00:16:30,875
A stop value or break ends the loop.

275
00:16:30,895 --> 00:16:34,247
And a trace table shows every value on
every pass.

276
00:16:34,267 --> 00:16:38,889
And with AI: ask for a trace, then run the
same answers yourself.

277
00:16:39,487 --> 00:16:40,639
One last check.

278
00:16:40,659 --> 00:16:41,812
N starts at 10.

279
00:16:41,832 --> 00:16:46,816
While n is greater than 0, the loop prints
n, then takes away 3.

280
00:16:46,836 --> 00:16:48,615
How many numbers print?

281
00:16:59,980 --> 00:17:00,143
D.

282
00:17:00,163 --> 00:17:02,060
Four: 10, 7, 4 and 1.

283
00:17:02,080 --> 00:17:05,437
Then n is minus 2, and the loop ends.

284
00:17:06,053 --> 00:17:07,348
Mission complete!

285
00:17:07,368 --> 00:17:10,673
You earned 100 XP and the Loop Tamer
badge.

286
00:17:12,917 --> 00:17:14,467
Now it is your turn.

287
00:17:14,487 --> 00:17:18,237
The practice file has eight tasks, worth
170 XP.

288
00:17:18,267 --> 00:17:20,947
Three warm-up tasks, worth 10 XP each.

289
00:17:20,967 --> 00:17:25,457
Three apply tasks, worth 20 each,
including a savings goal.

290
00:17:25,500 --> 00:17:28,042
And two stretch tasks, worth 40 each.

291
00:17:28,062 --> 00:17:31,436
In the last one, an AI's kettle timer
never ends.

292
00:17:31,456 --> 00:17:32,890
Trace it, and fix it.

293
00:17:32,933 --> 00:17:35,006
Trace each loop before you run it.

294
00:17:35,026 --> 00:17:37,653
Try each task before you open the
solution.

295
00:17:38,250 --> 00:17:43,349
To serve tables 1 to 3, Asha needed a
counter, a condition and a line that

296
00:17:43,369 --> 00:17:44,110
changes it.

297
00:17:44,130 --> 00:17:47,500
Three places to make a mistake, as you saw
today.

298
00:17:47,533 --> 00:17:52,336
In Chapter 8, for loops and range repeat a
block a set number of times, with no

299
00:17:52,356 --> 00:17:53,558
counter of your own.

300
00:17:53,578 --> 00:17:56,793
And a for loop can go through every letter
of a word.

301
00:17:56,833 --> 00:17:58,549
Do the warm-up tasks now.

302
00:17:58,569 --> 00:18:00,493
They take about ten minutes.

303
00:18:00,533 --> 00:18:02,240
See you in Chapter 8.
